'''3.''' Light; unsubstantial; trifling; worthless.
{{smaller block|Thus has he — and many more of the same breed that I know the drossy age dotes on — only got the tune of the time and outward habit of encounter: a kind of ''yesty'' collection, which carries them through and through the most fond and winnowed opinions.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, v. 2. 199.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Knowledge with him is idle, if it strain
Above the compass of his ''yesty'' brain.</poem>
{{right|''Drayton'', Moon-Calf.}}}}

'''yeat†''', ''n.'' Same as ''yate'', ''gate''<sup>1</sup>.
{{smaller block|<poem>And, or the porter was at the ''yeat'',
The boy was in the ha’.</poem>
{{right|''Lady Maisry'' (Child’s Ballads, II. 84).}}}}

'''yedd†''', ''v. i.'' [ME. ''ȝedden'', ''ȝeddien'', < AS. ''geddian'', ''gyddian'', ''giddian'', speak, sing, < ''gedd'', ''gidd'', a song.] To speak; sing. ''Piers Plowman'' (A), i. 138.

'''yedding†''', ''n.'' [ME., also ''yeddynge'', < AS. ''geddyng'', ''giddung''; verbal n. of ''geddian'', sing: see ''yedd'', ''v.''] A popular tale or romance, or a song embodying a popular tale or romance.
{{smaller block|Of ''yeddinges'' he bar utterly the prys.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Gen. Prol. to C. T., l. 237.}}}}

'''yede<sup>1</sup>†''', '''yode†'''. [ME. ''yede'', ''ȝede'', ''ȝode'', < AS. ''eode'' (= Goth. ''iddja''), pret. of ''gān'', go: see ''go''.] Obsolete irregular preterits of ''go''.
{{smaller block|<poem>Sethen ''ȝede'' to sitte same to solas & to pleie
At a wid windowe that was in the chaumber.</poem>
{{right|''William of Palerne'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 3672.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Two or three of his messages ''yeden''
For Pandarus. {{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, ii. 936.}}</poem>}}
{{smaller block|<poem>To mete hir ''ȝode'' mani baroun,
with grete and faire processioun.</poem>}}

