{{smaller block|{{right|''Holy Rood'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 115.}}

<poem>His army dry-foot through them ''yod.''</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I. x. 53.}}

<poem>One while this little boy he ''yode'',
Another while he ran.</poem>
{{right|''Childe Maurice'' (Child’s Ballads, II. 314).}}

<poem>Along the bankes of many silver streames
Thou with him ''yodest.''</poem>
{{right|''L. Bryskett'', Pastorall Aeglogue.}}

<poem>In other pace than forth he ''yode'',
Return’d Lord Marmion.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', Marmion, iii. 31.}}}}

¶'''yede<sup>2</sup>†''', ''v. i.'' [Also ''yead''; a false pres. tense and inf. formed from the pret. ''yede, yode'': see ''yede''<sup>1</sup>.] To go; proceed. [Rare and erroneous.]

{{smaller block|<poem>Then badd the knight this lady ''yede'' aloof,
And to an hill herselfe withdraw asyde.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I. xi. 5.}}

Years ''yead'' away, and faces fair deflower. {{right|''Drant.''}}}}

'''yeder†''', ''a.'' [ME. ''ȝedir''; cf. AS. ''ædre, edre'', quickly.] Quick. ''Wars of Alexander'', l. 5042.

'''yederly†''', ''adv.'' [ME. ''ȝederly, ȝederli''; < ''yeder'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] Quickly; at once.

{{smaller block|<poem>For I ȝelde me ''ȝederly'', & ȝeȝe after grace,
& that is the best, be my dome, for me by-houeȝ nede.</poem>
{{right|''Sir Gawayne and the Green Knight'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1215.}}}}

'''yeel''' (yēl), ''n.'' A dialectal form of ''eel.''

'''yeeld†''', ''v.'' A Middle English spelling of ''yield.''

'''yeep†''', ''a.'' Same as ''yep.''

'''yeffell†''', ''adv.'' An obsolete dialectal form of ''evil.''

