'''yeldring''' (yel′dring), ''n.'' [Also ''yeldrin, yoldring, yoldrin, yorling'', etc., in numerous variant forms based on ''yellow''.] Same as ''yowley''. [Scotch.]

'''yeldrock''' (yel′dro̤k), ''n.'' Same as ''yowley''. [Prov. Eng.]

'''yelk''' (yelk), ''n.'' A variant of ''yolk''.

'''yell'''<sup>1</sup> (yel), ''v.'' [< ME. ''yellen, ȝellen, ȝullen, ȝollen'', < AS. ''gellan, giellan, gyllan'', cry out, yell, resound, = D. ''gillen'', shriek, scream, = G. ''gellen'', resound, = Icel. ''gella'', also ''gjalla'' = Sw. ''gälla'' = Dan. ''gjælle, gjalde'', resound, ring; prob. akin to AS. ''galan'', sing: see ''gale''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''yawl''<sup>1</sup>, ''yowl''.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' To cry out with a sharp, loud noise; shriek; cry or scream as with agony, horror, or ferocity.
{{smaller block|Thay ''yelleden'' as feendes doon in helle.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Nun’s Priest’s Tale, l. 569.}}
Tho com the deuel ''ȝollynge'' uorth, [and] loude he gan grede<br>Alas nou is my myȝte ido euermo he sede.
{{right|''Holy Rood'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 44.}}
The night raven that still deadly ''yells''. {{right|''Spenser''.}}
The dogs did ''yell''. {{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., iv. 2. 60.}}
<poem>The throng’d arena shakes with shouts for more;
''Yells'' the mad crowd o’er entrails freshly torn.</poem>
{{right|''Byron'', Childe Harold, i. 68.}}
<poem>All the men and women in the hall
Rose, when they saw the dead man rise, and fled
''Yelling'' as from a spectre.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Geraint.}}}}
'''II.''' ''trans.'' To utter with a yell.
{{smaller block|<poem>As if it felt with Scotland, and ''yell’d'' out
Like syllable of dolour.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, iv. 3. 7.}}
Some boy, galloping for life upon the road, ''yells'' to him the sudden news, and is gone.
{{right|''W. M. Baker'', New Timothy, p. 258.}}}}
